package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setPhyrexiaAllWillBeOne = StorySection(
    "one", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One",
    "A handful of planeswalkers fell through nine spheres of living metal to plant a bomb at the root of a tree, and the tree bloomed anyway.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "one-new-phyrexia", "New Phyrexia",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-third-act", "The Third Act",
                    listOf(
                        "This set came out on February 10, 2023, a week later than first planned, with prerelease weekend running from the third to the ninth and the digital release landing on the seventh. Its code is ONE. It was the ninety-fifth expansion in the game's history, 271 cards in the main set, and the third chapter of a four-set run that began with Dominaria United, went back in time for The Brothers' War, and ended with March of the Machine.",
                        "In the story it is the moment the war turns. On Dominaria the planeswalkers had won a battle against Sheoldred and lost almost everything else. The Sylex was destroyed, [Karn](who/karn) was taken, and Ajani Goldmane had been revealed as a Phyrexian sleeper. Every world in the Multiverse now sat in New Phyrexia's sights. So for once the heroes did not wait to be invaded. They went to the enemy's home and tried to end it there.",
                        "The set shows the plane from the inside, often from the Phyrexians' own point of view, and its people fight each other almost as hard as they fight anyone else. The cards are full of the strike team's losses. Ten planeswalkers were printed in it, and five of them are compleated, which tells anyone who opens a pack how the story is going to end before they read a word.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-karns-world", "Karn's World",
                    listOf(
                        "The plane was never meant to be a nightmare. [Karn](who/karn), the silver golem, built it as Argentum, a quiet artificial world of metal lit by small stars and walked only by golems. He left it in the care of a golem named Memnarch and went off to see the Multiverse. What he did not know was that a little Phyrexian oil had come with him unnoticed, and it found Memnarch first. The warden went mad, renamed the world Mirrodin after the Mirari, and filled it with creatures snatched from other planes, hoping one of them would carry a spark he could steal.",
                        "The Mirrans who grew up there were people of flesh and metal together. Elves lived in the copper Tangle, leonin and Auriok in the Razor Fields, goblins in the Oxidda mountains, and five suns of raw mana circled overhead. After Memnarch fell, the oil kept spreading in the dark. A new Phyrexia grew under Mirrodin's skin, in all five colours of mana at once, and when it broke the surface it won. Venser gave his life to heal Karn, Elspeth and Koth fought beside the Mirrans, and Mirrodin was lost all the same.",
                        "Five praetors carved the conquered world between them, and for years they quarreled. Then [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn), the white praetor of unity, made war on the others. Jin-Gitaxias and Vorinclex bent the knee, Sheoldred and Urabrask had their realms laid waste, and Norn named herself the Mother of Machines. By the time of this set the plane is hers, and she has made it into a copy of the nine-layered Phyrexia that [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth) once ruled.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-outer-spheres", "The Outer Spheres",
                    listOf(
                        "The Phyrexians built New Phyrexia in layers, a sphere inside a sphere inside a sphere. The outermost is {The Monumental Facade}, a thin shell raised no more than a hundred feet above the old surface, made from scraps of the Glimmervoid and covered in temples and monuments to the praetors. It is the only sphere that still sees the five suns, dim through fog. Its ground is a desert of fine metal dust that lulls any living thing that lies down in it, and its shell cracks and falls in all the time.",
                        "Beneath it lies {Mirrex}, which is all that is left of Mirrodin's real surface. The Phyrexians gutted it to build everything else, and cut off from the suns it became a wasteland where little grows and the hungry Mirran Resistance hides among the ruins. Below that is the Furnace Layer, {The Autonomous Furnace}, the first sphere the Phyrexians ever made and the red praetor Urabrask's domain. Rivers of molten metal feed forges that build ever larger machines, and furnace bosses run their own sections like rival foremen.",
                        "Strangest of all, the Furnace is where most of the surviving Mirrans live. Urabrask's red Phyrexians still feel things, and they let the resistance camp in their sphere as long as nobody looks too closely. The Mirrans move between spheres through the lacunae, old tunnels to the plane's core that play games with gravity, so that a traveller jumps in, finds herself standing on the wall, and walks down into the dark.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("In Urabrask's domain, mighty forges echo day and night with the clangor of the Great Work.", "The Autonomous Furnace"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-inner-spheres", "The Inner Spheres",
                    listOf(
                        "Under the Furnace grows {The Hunter Maze}, Vorinclex's sphere, where Mirrodin's forests were transplanted and trees of oxidized metal rise so high that neither floor nor ceiling can be seen. Everything there hunts everything else. Below it is {The Surgical Bay}, Jin-Gitaxias's laboratories on islands in a sea of quicksilver and glistening oil, all of it watched by mechanical eyes. Then come {The Dross Pits}, Sheoldred's sphere of acid pools and necrogen fumes, where the Steel Thanes fight an endless war of succession and the loser of a fight in her coliseum is harvested for parts.",
                        "Deeper still is {The Fair Basilica}, white and red and built in Norn's image. Its walls are made of ossified Phyrexians she judged perfect, crimson capillaries glow in its marble floors, and to be turned to bone and set into her throne is the greatest honour the sphere has to give. Under the throne a stair winds down into {The Mycosynth Gardens}, the old core of Mirrodin, where columns of fungus grow unchecked. Norn forbids anyone to enter without her word, praetors included.",
                        "At the very centre is {The Seedcore}. It was once Memnarch's Panopticon and later the throne room where Karn was kept as the Father of Machines. Now it holds the thing the whole plane was rebuilt to serve, the Invasion Tree, a copy of Norn's throne woven from its roots and its branches reaching up into the Gardens and beyond. Nine spheres, five praetors, one Mother. The plane is a temple, and the tree is what it worships.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("In Elesh Norn's domain, the gospel of unity is the highest law.", "The Fair Basilica"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Set", "Scars of Mirrodin"),
                StorySource("Story", "Assault on New Phyrexia, Episode 2: Unstable Foundations, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexia (plane)"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-the-descent", "The Descent",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-strike-team", "The Strike Team",
                    listOf(
                        "The plan was [Karn](who/karn)'s before it was anyone else's. Realmbreaker, the Invasion Tree, was growing in the Seedcore, and once its branches broke through the Blind Eternities it would carry New Phyrexia to every world at once. A sylex set off among its roots might destroy it while it was still trapped on one plane. Ajani had destroyed the only sylex on Dominaria, so Saheeli Rai built a perfect replica, and [Teferi](who/teferi) sent his spirit back to the Brothers' War to learn how to use it. He saw [Urza](who/urza) set off the first one and learned that it was a person, not a spell, that made a sylex work.",
                        "[Jace Beleren](who/jace) did the gathering. He sent planeswalkers home to shore up their own worlds and picked a strike force for the descent, Vraska, Kaya, the Wanderer, Kaito Shizuki, Nissa Revane, Lukka, [Nahiri](who/nahiri), Tyvar Kell and Elspeth Tirel, with Koth waiting below among the resistance. He named Elspeth leader, because she had commanded armies and because she had already fought for Mirrodin once and lost.",
                        "Jace carried the sylex and knew how to fire it. Kaya had been trained as the backup. Jace had also laid a mental link across the whole team before they left, so that wherever the fighting took them they could still speak to each other. It was a good plan, made by careful people who had studied what little was known about the plane. Nobody alive had ever seen a fully compleated world from the inside.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-barrier", "The Barrier",
                    listOf(
                        "[Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) knew they were coming. She had wrapped New Phyrexia in a planar shield, and crossing it felt like slamming into a wall of electrostatic wind. It knocked the planeswalkers senseless, broke Jace's mind link, stripped them of gear and scattered them across the plane two layers higher than they meant to land. Kaito woke on the Monumental Facade believing he was lying on a beach like the nicer ones on Kamigawa, sinking happily into warm sand, until the Wanderer slapped him hard enough to crack his cheek. The sand was metal dust, the waves were the sound of a battle, and his sword and his little drone Himoto were gone.",
                        "The barrier was worse for the Wanderer, whose hold on any world was weak at the best of times. She kept guttering in and out like a candle, unable to speak in more than broken words. [Nahiri](who/nahiri) had already finished off a Phyrexian with a storm of the plane's own sand. She brushed off a scratch on the back of her neck. Her blood was still red, she said, and nothing she could not walk off.",
                        "On the way across the dunes they found a stone statue of an elf hung between iron pillars in living cables, and Nahiri saw that the stone was a Zendikari hedron. Kaito pelted the cables with shrapnel, Nahiri cut them with her knives, and the statue dropped to the ground, turned back to flesh and swung at the nearest face. It was Tyvar Kell, Prince of Kaldheim, who could take on the nature of whatever he touched and had been held fast in stone. He bowed to the Wanderer, and she tried to answer and could not.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("New Phyrexia's planar defenses scattered the Planeswalker strike team on arrival. Elesh Norn knew they were coming.", "Planar Disruption"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-melira", "Melira",
                    listOf(
                        "At a ramshackle camp of Auriok and leonin a wiry woman with short red hair and a staff of light came out to meet them. She was Melira, a healer, and Koth had sent her to watch for them. She told them where they were, on the shell the Phyrexians had built so the Mirrans would never see their suns again, and then she slammed her staff into the ground and set off charges under their feet. A ten foot square of the Facade fell in. Nahiri caught the falling slabs and rode them down like a raft while Melira laughed. If they could not handle a little fall, she said, they were never going to win.",
                        "Below lay Mirrex, the real Mirrodin, withered from lack of light. Melira could walk it without a guard because she was immune to phyresis, the one person on the plane the oil could not take. She led them to a lacuna by Lowlight and jumped in, and they followed, standing on the walls of a tunnel that fell toward the Furnace. The Wanderer was already gone. She had stayed exactly as long as it took to see her friend Kaito out of danger.",
                        "Nahiri hung back. The scratch on her neck throbbed. When she peeled the bandage off she found smooth skin and a short slick spur growing out of it, and her fingers came away gleaming with oil. She was infected, and she told no one. Far above them the Wanderer flickered back onto the Facade, found the camp razed and the hole in the ground empty, and understood she had come back too late to warn them.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-furnace-camp", "The Furnace Camp",
                    listOf(
                        "Elspeth had woken alone and was cutting through a pack of Phyrexians when Kaya's purple blade came through one of them from behind. Elspeth could not be infected, and Kaya could, so Elspeth fussed over every scratch. A troll named Thrun broke a hole in the Facade and dropped them a rope ladder, and they walked down the white lacuna, Kaya carrying a little tanuki drone she had found beside her when she woke. They let go at the bottom and the lacuna's magic set them down in the Furnace Layer, on a shelf of black rock among soot-dark tents.",
                        "A voice like a mountain shouted her name. It was Koth, the Vulshok geomancer, whom Elspeth had thought dead since the fall of Mirrodin, and she ran into his arms laughing. Tyvar was there, and Kaito and Nahiri, and Jace, who had walked over the surface alone. Kaito got his sword back from Kaya, and his drone leapt onto his shoulder. Vraska was missing. Nissa had been with Jace when they landed, until a trap took her away as if she had been made to planeswalk. Nobody knew where Lukka was. The Wanderer had come and gone. Barely begun, the strike team was already half of what it had been, and the Phyrexians had clearly been ready for them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Well, aren't you a sight for sore eyes!\" said Koth. \"I was about to say the same, my friend,\" Elspeth replied.", "Resistance Reunited"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-hexgold", "Hexgold and Secrets",
                    listOf(
                        "Melira had a question no one had answered. What would the sylex do to Mirrodin? She had Nahiri shape a little ball of stone and wrap shell after shell around it, and named each one as it formed, the Seedcore, the Gardens, the Basilica, the four spheres and the two shells above, until the whole plane hung turning in the air. Then she asked Jace to look her in the eye. He admitted he did not know. The blast might tear the plane apart. That was the answer she wanted. Koth had already told her the risk. She would help them as long as they did not lie.",
                        "The resistance shared what it had. Their gear was treated with hexgold, plates of Glimmervoid metal scavenged from Mirrex and refined with blinkmoth serum, which made a blade bite harder against the compleated and gave a little protection from the oil. Kaito honed every piece of his sword on it and had Himoto dusted with the powder. Tyvar asked for a piece of the Glimmervoid itself and turned it between his fingers like a coin. Koth announced that he and a demolition team were coming too. He did not like problems with only one solution.",
                        "In a quiet corner [Nahiri](who/nahiri) cut down to the spur in her neck and sprinkled hexgold into the wound, and her skin spat it back out. Melira had already guessed. She could cure her, she said, but it would leave her helpless for days. Nahiri would not lose the time. So they agreed to fight now and heal later, and the strike team boarded hand-pumped carts and rolled down a Mirran tunnel toward the Dross Pits, Elspeth last of all.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Story", "Assault on New Phyrexia, Episode 1: Uncontrolled Descent, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Assault on New Phyrexia, Episode 2: Unstable Foundations, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexian Invasion"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-the-dross-pits", "The Dross Pits",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-necrogen-and-halo", "Necrogen and Halo",
                    listOf(
                        "The carts dropped out of the tunnel under a sky of leprous cloud, among pools of glowing green necrogen that made everyone look already dead. The cart operators told Elspeth that everything here rots and that anyone who breathes the fumes too long rots with it. Koth knew a tunnel nearby that ran straight to the Fair Basilica. The seven Steel Thanes ruled this sphere and had never been united, and four of them, Roxith, Geth, Vraan and Sheoldred herself, had thrown in with Urabrask's rebellion against [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn). The rest would be busy grabbing territory. The chaos was their cover.",
                        "Elspeth passed around bottles of Halo, the bright citrus drink made from angelic essence on New Capenna, which Phyrexians cannot bear. It would hold off the necrogen for a while. [Jace](who/jace) swallowed his and fell backwards onto the cart, twitching, his pulse running wild. Melira said it was not phyresis. Then he sat up crackling with blue light and tried to run off across the waste.",
                        "The Halo had cleared his head, and with his head clear the old link came back for one voice. Vraska had made it down here, he said, and she was alone and she was afraid, and he would hear her anywhere. Tyvar held his arm and told him a force divided was no force at all. Jace tore free and walked off. He had the sylex, so the others followed him, and the safe road through the Dross Pits was abandoned behind them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("This was once a peaceful spring, but I wouldn't advise taking a sip.", "Melira, Venerated Rotpriest"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-coliseum", "Sheoldred's Coliseum",
                    listOf(
                        "Jace hid them inside an illusion as they crossed a battlefield, so that the black and red Phyrexians tearing into each other saw nothing at all. Ahead rose Sheoldred's Coliseum under the wings of a ribcage too big for any living thing. Koth had been inside once. Nobody, he said, comes out of that place alive and unchanged. Jace ran for the gate, [Nahiri](who/nahiri) ran after the sylex, and Kaya ran after Nahiri.",
                        "In the pit stood Vraska, bleeding from a dozen wounds, holding something in place at her middle with one hand, with petrified Phyrexians and a dozen dead Mirrans around her. The planeswalkers waded in. Nahiri cut down two before they could turn, Koth tore a claw off a creature welded together from smaller corpses and drove it through another's throat, and Kaito pulled a sheet of oil off Tyvar's metal back and flung it into a Phyrexian's eyes. When it was done, Vraska stepped away from Jace's reaching hands.",
                        "She was too far gone. Melira could feel it from where she stood. Nahiri offered her a clean death while she was still herself, and Jace swore he would kill anyone who touched her. Kaito said it plainly. Jace could stay with her if he chose. He could not stay with the sylex. Nahiri's knives cut his pack straps, Kaya caught the bag, and the others ran, leaving the two of them alone in the bowl.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("The dull, creeping pain of phyresis curdled in Vraska's blood. She knew as soon as it took her, she would betray everything—even Jace.", "Vraska's Fall"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-one-more-day", "One More Day",
                    listOf(
                        "Jace asked Vraska to close her eyes, and when she opened them they were standing on a sunny avenue on Ravnica under a perfect cloudless sky. He was dressed for an afternoon walk. He could not save her from Phyrexia, he told her, but he could give her one more day. They walked between the guild halls and the great museums with her head on his shoulder, inside his dream of the future they might have had.",
                        "She told him to go before Phyrexia reached her mind, and he would not. Then she told him it was too late for him as well. In the Dross Pits the oil hangs in the air above the necrogen, and he had breathed it. As they kissed, something stabbed into the palm of his right hand. She had grown a long barbed tail like a scorpion's, and she had filled him with glistening oil. The illusion shattered.",
                        "Vraska laughed, and dedicated the deed to the glory of Phyrexia, and turned her gaze on him for the first time. Jace threw his burning arm across his face and ran, straight into Tyvar's metal side. The others were retreating back through the gate ahead of a closing tide of Phyrexians. The card that remembers the moment, {Vraska's Fall}, makes an opponent give up a creature or planeswalker and take a poison counter, which is about what happened to everyone in that arena.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-nahiris-sacrifice", "Nahiri's Sacrifice",
                    listOf(
                        "There was no way out. Nahiri's bandage had come loose in the fight, and she tore it off and showed them the bony growth on her spine. Then she reached into the sphere itself. One by one her floating knives dropped to the ground as her sword blazed brighter, the air shimmered with heat, and the coliseum began to warp and crack. The spurs spread across her as she pulled power from Phyrexia, her skin split into veins of burning red, and her eyes went black like dead coals.",
                        "She looked at Jace across the broken field and told him not to let it be for nothing, to finish the job. Then she swung, and cleaved the plane. The whole coliseum fell through the floor of the Dross Pits into the sphere below. When the dust cleared the others crawled out of the rubble onto white porcelain under a hole in a silver sky, alive. Elspeth's last Halo had smashed and drained away as rainbow mist. The sylex was whole. Nahiri was nowhere to be found.",
                        "Phyrexians poured down through the hole and began fighting each other on the ceiling, and up the walls came the white shells of the Fair Basilica's guardians. Bright on the horizon, wings spread, Atraxa was fighting the black invaders of her mistress's domain. Jace held up his arm. Under the burn the skin had split, and something gleamed through that was not blood. He asked Kaya for the sylex back. If he was dying, he said, he might as well be the one to fire it.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Nahiri's last free breath became a defiant roar as she tore apart Sheoldred's Coliseum.", "Nahiri's Sacrifice"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Story", "Assault on New Phyrexia, Episode 3: Inconceivable Losses, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexian Invasion"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-the-seedcore", "The Seedcore",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-white-sphere", "The White Sphere",
                    listOf(
                        "They had landed on a high bridge in the Fair Basilica, a lattice of alabaster platforms joined by long spans of crimson sinew, everything red against white like blood across a beach. A faint hymn hung in the air as if the buildings themselves were singing. Melira told them that [Nahiri](who/nahiri) had confided in her back at the Furnace, and that she could have saved her, at the cost of leaving her behind for days. Nahiri had chosen not to be left.",
                        "Kaito said he hoped she was dead. Nahiri was probably the strongest of them, he explained, and he did not want to meet her on the other side of a battlefield. Tyvar said quietly that sometimes it is better to grieve a companion than to risk fighting one. Kaya thanked them sourly for that thought.",
                        "Beside the bridge loomed a giant of red and white with a single empty socket where its eye should be. Koth said [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) had her favourites ossified, turned to bone and built into the sphere, and that he had seen such statues wake and kill. It did not move. They passed into the empty foyer of Norn's altar between columns of frozen bodies and walls that wept sinew and showed row on row of human teeth, and felt with every step that they were walking into a trap.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Only Elesh Norn's most loyal servants are granted the honor of becoming part of her throne.", "Ossification"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-invasion-tree", "The Invasion Tree",
                    listOf(
                        "Under Norn's throne a stair wound down into the Mycosynth Gardens, its walls turning from white metal to the pebbled grey of the fungus that had taken Mirrodin in the first place. Melira led them to the gate of the Seedcore, a heap of fungal strands like gutted entrails that opened into a mouth of waving tendrils. It infected anything it touched. Melira walked up to it and wiped the oil off. The rest of them could not.",
                        "Tyvar had an answer. He would spread his Glimmervoid metal over all of them at once while they passed through, and Kaito would strip the oil off afterward. The smell of green growing things filled the air, the smell of Kaldheim, and one by one they turned to metal, [Jace](who/jace) last, his wound almost refusing. The tendrils left streaks of oil on their hard skins and did nothing else. On the far side Tyvar let go, and Kaito rolled the oil into a ball and tossed it off the bridge.",
                        "Then Tyvar saw the tree. Its bark was white porcelain split with agonized red, it wept oil for sap, and from its highest branches long white ships hung waiting. To a prince of Kaldheim it was a perversion of his world's soul. As they watched, light flashed through the upper branches in a lattice, and then again, brighter, until the colours of the Blind Eternities broke through. Kaya said what everyone could see. They were too late. Jace collapsed, and Tyvar picked him up and carried him.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("The Invasion Tree broke through the Blind Eternities and sent Phyrexian perfection coursing across the Multiverse.", "All Will Be One"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-karn-in-the-roots", "Karn in the Roots",
                    listOf(
                        "Inside the roots was a domed chamber, and on a low dais at its centre lay [Karn](who/karn), broken open and spread across the platform like a dissection. He turned his head at their footsteps and told them they should not have come. The Phyrexians had rejected their Father of Machines. When Elspeth told him they had made another sylex, he told them to take it to the root source and set it off there, and said he would take the burden from them if he could.",
                        "Jace answered that it was too late for Karn and too late for him too, and showed his arm. He hobbled on toward the far doorway, and Tyvar and Kaya followed. Koth, Melira and the demolition team stayed behind to blast Karn free of his restraints and wipe the oil from him. Karn told Elspeth to go and then get off the plane and find a better place to make a last stand. She said no. There would be no more running.",
                        "The last bridge was long and white and veined with red, and it ended at a copy of Norn's altar woven out of roots. Overhead the trunk rose through a tear into the Blind Eternities, and along gangways to the white ships shuffled endless ranks of Phyrexians. What the strike team had fought on the way down had only been what the Phyrexians thought they were worth. The real army had been down here the whole time.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-ajani-and-tibalt", "Ajani and Tibalt",
                    listOf(
                        "Footsteps came along the bridge behind them, calm as a stroll in a park. It was Ajani, in red and white armour that had grown out of his body, carrying a huge double axe with the blades turned in Norn's honour, and beside him Tibalt, all bone plates and braided sinew, his forked tail split to the base into two stingers that dripped oil. Ajani smiled at Elspeth with his old voice and told her how glad he was that she had survived to join him. No more difference, no more pain. All will be One.",
                        "Tibalt called Tyvar a princeling too stupid to be afraid. Tyvar sent Kaito on with Jace and Kaya and stayed with Elspeth on the bridge. The two compleated planeswalkers had been among the strongest friends and the worst enemies the heroes had ever had, and now they stood together in the livery of the same mother. Glimmervoid metal rippled across Tyvar's skin, Elspeth raised her sword at the leonin who had taught her, and behind them, a moment later, the screaming began.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Elspeth, my friend, the Multiverse has answered my prayers to bring you here so that I may gather you into my pride and forever protect you.\"", "Compleat Devotion"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Story", "Assault on New Phyrexia, Episode 4: Impossible Odds, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Realmbreaker"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-bring-the-ending", "Bring the Ending",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-last-argument", "The Last Argument",
                    listOf(
                        "[Jace](who/jace), Kaya and Kaito went on through a little copy of Norn's citadel full of golden light with no source, under a skylight like a fly's wing smeared with the red mist of the launching ships. Wires were growing out of Jace's arm and weaving a basket around it. Kaya could not find a single ghost on a plane that had seen so much death. Phyrexia, she thought, does not let its victims go even then. Kaito found a hole in the floor that dropped to a white disc hanging over the root of the tree. It was as close as they would get.",
                        "Jace sat down beside the sylex, and Kaya stopped him. The tree had connected. If the blast ran up its branches it might wreck every world it touched, Vryn and Ixalan and Ravnica among them, and any planeswalker caught in transit. Kaito had come to save Kamigawa, not destroy it. Jace said that if Phyrexia had reached those worlds, they were already lost, that he was dying, and that there was no other way. Kaya pulled a dagger. Kaito drew his sword beside her.",
                        "Then there were three Jaces. Kaya could see spirits, and only one of them had a soul, and Kaito's shuriken caught that one in the arm and the false images died. She walked up to him and grabbed the sylex in both hands, and it melted like mist between her fingers. That had been an illusion too. Jace stood across the real sylex with wires spidering over his face, and apologised to both of them, and then to everyone, and disappeared behind his own magic.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-doubt-and-hope", "Doubt and Hope",
                    listOf(
                        "On the bridge Ajani pushed Elspeth back and back without ever swinging, talking to her the way you talk a child out of a bad idea. When she refused him he said she did not need to be alive to join Phyrexia, and the fight began in earnest. Beside them Tibalt stabbed at Tyvar with his twin tails and taunted him, the last prince of Kaldheim, a man chosen by a greatness he could never be worthy of. Then Tibalt turned his smile on Elspeth and breathed out doubt, the greatest weapon of all.",
                        "It poured into her. Ajani's fall was her fault, Mirrodin's fall was her fault, every loss on the way down was hers. Ajani knocked the sword from her hands and swung for her neck, and her blade came up between them anyway, raised by something underneath all her doubts, the child who had once defied Elesh Norn on a plane without hope. Tyvar caught Tibalt's tail and let his Glimmervoid metal flow out across the devil's body. His magic smothers whatever it covers, and doubt cannot touch what it cannot reach.",
                        "Hope blazed out of Elspeth like white light. Doubt is nothing, she said, it does not change what is right, and she slammed her hilt into the back of Ajani's neck and laid him out cold. Tyvar told her to go. As she ran, she saw him drive both of Tibalt's own barbs into the place his heart should have been and shove him off the bridge onto the span below.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"When they write songs about this battle, I will make sure your name is forgotten, devil.\"", "Tyvar's Stand"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-sylex", "The Sylex",
                    listOf(
                        "Hidden from sight, Jace cut his forehead with his thumbnail and poured into the bowl everything he had. His grief and his fury, the suffering of all Mirrodin, regret for the Multiverse and his love for Vraska. He spoke the old words because they felt right, the same words [Urza](who/urza) had spoken long ago, which [Teferi](who/teferi) had witnessed and Kaya had seen through Teferi and Jace through Kaya, one unbroken line. Wipe the land clear. Bring the ending. Light began to climb the inside of the bowl.",
                        "Kaya cried out and Kaito stepped in front of her. Neither of them saw Elspeth drop through the hole in the ceiling. Jace turned toward her with his eyes blazing blue, and in that instant she understood what he meant to do and what it would do to the Multiverse. She drove her sword through him without hesitation, shoved him aside with the blade still in him, and took the sylex in her own hands.",
                        "She had time to look at Kaya and Kaito as the light crested the rim. Then there was a sharp crack, and Elspeth was gone, and the sylex with her, bound for somewhere beyond the Blind Eternities. Tyvar, bloodied and flesh again, dropped down beside them a moment later and found her missing. The bomb that might have wiped out a dozen worlds had gone off in the nothing between them instead.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Faced with a blast that would level not only New Phyrexia, but also a dozen innocent worlds, Elspeth consigned it—and herself—to the nothingness in between.", "Vanish into Eternity"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-all-will-be-one", "All Will Be One",
                    listOf(
                        "Great booms shook the room, and the trunk of Realmbreaker pulsed with oil-slick light, flickering through night and day as fast as a heartbeat. The tree was fully awake and sending across the Multiverse. Above them its branches winked out one by one, still joined to the tree and somewhere else at once, each loaded with Phyrexians walking the Omenpaths to another world. Tyvar said only that he had seen hope that day, and they had not failed.",
                        "A wall opened like an eye. Into the room walked [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn), skeletally thin, flensed red tissue and porcelain white, and behind her a squad of Phyrexians with Tamiyo among them, her softness sharpened to blades. Norn welcomed the weary travellers to Phyrexia and smiled at Jace's body, and it rose and walked to her, leaving Elspeth's sword on the floor for Kaito to snatch up.",
                        "Then her ranks parted for [Nahiri](who/nahiri), who had not survived her fall. Her hands were blades now and molten metal glowed through the cracks in her skin. Behind her came Nissa on a forest of whip-thin cables, oil streaked across her face, nothing of the quiet animist left. They had come from the same world and had never been friends, Norn said, and now they were sisters. Tyvar said no, Kaito said he was good, and Kaya told her to go to hell. Norn clicked her claws together, and the [invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse) began.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Kaya watched in horror as the illusion vanished from her hands. Jace held the real Sylex—and had activated it before phyresis claimed his mind.", "Bring the Ending"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Story", "Assault on New Phyrexia, Episode 5: Inevitable Resolutions, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexian Invasion"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-the-hunter-maze", "The Hunter Maze",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-lukkas-landing", "Lukka's Landing",
                    listOf(
                        "Lukka stepped out of the Blind Eternities into heat, rot and the shriek of unseen beasts, and knew at once that he was not where he was meant to be. He had risen to captain the Specials among Ikoria's Coppercoats, a soldier who could bond his mind to monsters, and he had done terrible things on Ikoria and at Strixhaven. He had expected [Jace](who/jace) to come for him over them. Instead Jace had recruited him, because the strike team needed a soldier and because Phyrexia threatened Ikoria, and Lukka still cared too much about home to say no.",
                        "He had landed in the Hunter Maze, Vorinclex's forest of metal and flesh, where trees ooze red mucus when they are cut and leaves curl into fists to hide from a stranger. Rather than risk planeswalking somewhere worse he threw his harpoon into the branches and climbed. Above him he found Nissa Revane and the Wanderer fighting a twisted Phyrexian centaur, and saw a second beast creeping along a branch above them to pounce.",
                        "He reached out with the eludha, the bond he could forge with another creature, and squeezed its metal-bright mind until it froze. Nissa and the Wanderer finished the first beast, and then the Wanderer flickered away again. Nissa looked at the monster now standing meekly at Lukka's side, and at him, with the look he had been getting since the first time he bonded with a monster. She wanted to destroy it. He promised her that if he got so much as a stomachache, he would end it.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-centaur-hulk", "The Centaur Hulk",
                    listOf(
                        "The centaur hulk carried them up. It stretched its body across the gaps between boughs for them to walk over like a bridge, and it climbed bare trunks with its razor forelimbs while Nissa clung on behind Lukka with her heels in its sides. Things went wrong in small ways. A winged Phyrexian dove at Nissa and he pinned it to a tree with his harpoon. When the hulk climbed it rooted itself inside him, and when it let go a few tendrils stayed behind in his belly and kissed his fingertips like sea anemones.",
                        "It did not hurt. He felt stronger and more like himself than he had in years. His knees and hips felt rebuilt into a better shape, and his fingers grew sharp as metal. When a mother-beast full of leech-like young opened its many mouths under their feet and swallowed the hulk, he leapt into its maw and sliced it open with his bare hands to free his Phyrexian, and the hulk lay down at his feet in gratitude. Picking at a scab, he found maggot-like movement underneath and a gleam of metal bone, and marvelled that it did not hurt.",
                        "Somewhere along the way the Maze had begun to look beautiful to him, its breathing leaves and its flowers dripping black oil. Nissa wanted to planeswalk out. Lukka found he no longer quite remembered when their goal had changed from reaching the surface to reaching the centre. Then the Wanderer flickered in long enough to tell them that Vorinclex was close and recovered from his trip to Kaldheim, and to call it an opportunity, before she was dragged away again.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-glissa-and-vorinclex", "Glissa and Vorinclex",
                    listOf(
                        "At the heart of the Maze, under hanging skulls, Vorinclex was fighting a Phyrexian elf with a blade for an arm and copper cords for hair. It was Glissa, who had once lit the fifth sun of Mirrodin, and the two of them were laughing. Lukka could not tell whether they were fighting or playing. Nissa whispered that two of them could maybe take Vorinclex, but not both. Lukka charged.",
                        "He fought Glissa with his own claws and was happier than he had been since his sparring days in the Coppercoats. When he stumbled, his hulk rushed to save him, and its spine became his spine. He stood on its legs with a second pair of arms, his weapon finally a part of him. Nissa had cut Vorinclex a dozen times, but when the praetor rasped at Lukka to get the elf, she looked at him, and looked again, and backed away in horror. The Wanderer appeared between them, parried his blow and shouted at Nissa to run. Nissa ran.",
                        "Glissa told Lukka to stop, and he stopped. Find the elf, she told him, but do not kill her, because New Phyrexia would have a use for her in the war to come. He felt Vorinclex laughing beside him and Glissa's pleasure in his confidence, and he smiled, and went hunting. What use Phyrexia found for Nissa is told in [the Seedcore](one-bring-the-ending), and the card {Lukka, Bound to Ruin} shows the man who came out of the Maze.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Story", "Hard as Anger, Bright as Joy, by Langley Hyde, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexian Invasion"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-other-tales", "Other Tales",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-a-hollow-body", "A Hollow Body",
                    listOf(
                        "One of the set's side stories is told to a Phyrexian angel in the second person. Ixhel of the Fair Basilica, one of the angels Atraxa made in her own image, flies up a shaft into the Dross Pits with orders to kill Geth, the lich thane who sold protection in exchange for contracts. At a stone gate sealed by a puzzle of tones she nearly strangles a half-compleated aspirant named Belaxis, who begs for his life and then talks her through the puzzle. Ixhel has never thought to wonder why anyone would want to live.",
                        "Geth is waiting in a throne room with an empty copy of Norn's throne, a great spider body with only his rotting head still flesh. He bats her about and asks her what will hold her people together once they have burned down everything else. She drags her own throat along his pincer to break free, cuts off his head and catches it. Then she pins Belaxis, who helped her, on a spike of the Dominus of Pain, and when he asks her why, she tells him she is grateful.",
                        "In the Surgical Bay she builds something out of them, Geth's head, a body plated in green and silver, and two limbs that were once her own wings. She names him Vishgraz and brings him home. Atraxa looks on him with disgust and orders him destroyed. Ixhel takes him back to the shaft instead and sends him away, and calls him her first defiance. He became {Vishgraz, the Doomhive}, and she became {Ixhel, Scion of Atraxa}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"It seems someone forgot to read the fine print.\"", "Geth, Thane of Contracts"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-cinders", "Cinders",
                    listOf(
                        "Neyali leads a cell of the Mirran Resistance on the edge of the Quiet Furnace with her firebird Otharri. When her childhood friend Reyana goes missing on the march, Neyali finds her backing toward a ledge over magma before an aspirant with a scythe for an arm, and shoves the creature over the edge. Only afterward does Reyana tell her the aspirant was her mother. Her mother had been frightened all her life, Reyana says, and for the first time she had looked at peace. What if Phyrexia is right?",
                        "Reyana leaves a note and walks away to the Salvage Complex, where the compleated goblin Slobad recycles obsolete Phyrexians. Neyali follows with her bravest, and finds her friend chained alone in a pit beneath rows of husked bodies. It is a trap. Reyana's chains fall away, Slobad offers reunion with everyone they have lost, and the husband Saheena thought dead stands compleated at his side. Neyali trades herself for the three who came down with her, and is caged high above to watch.",
                        "Then talons click on the bars. Otharri burns through her cage and through cage after cage, singing, and Neyali's magic leaps onto every Mirran he frees as a skin of fire. Her cell, all of them, comes charging in to clear the way. Reyana turns her back on Neyali's outstretched hand and kneels for the oil. Neyali salutes her, and leads her people out. Those two became {Neyali, Suns' Vanguard} and {Otharri, Suns' Glory}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Once he fought to save Mirrodin. Now he fights to remake it.", "Slobad, Iron Goblin"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-a-man-of-parts", "A Man of Parts",
                    listOf(
                        "Tezzeret came back through the Planar Bridge carrying the charred endoskeleton of Rona, his flesh being eaten by the bridge that powered him. He blamed his failure on Dominaria on Rona and on Sheoldred's treachery, warned [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) about Elspeth, and asked for what he had been promised, a body of darksteel. She gave it to him in Jin-Gitaxias's laboratory, bound in the very seat where Tamiyo had been cut open and compleated. He woke stronger than he had ever been, and could not planeswalk. Jin-Gitaxias meant to turn his darksteel to blightsteel, and knew all about Tezzeret's plotting with Urabrask.",
                        "Tezzeret took hold of the mind of a captured scrapchief in a tank and drove it into the slab that drained his magic until he broke free, and then fought the praetor himself. Out among the planes, Aranzhur, Ilcae and Obsidias had fallen. On Alara he pressed a box of etherium on a terrified boy named Estel and told him to run for Bant. On Kamigawa he broke a gang of bikers to reach a safehouse and left Tamiyo's son Nashi alive, with a warning that his mother would come for him.",
                        "He ended on a nameless desert plane before his old tower of the Infinite Consortium. Beleren and his friends would fight Norn to the end, he reckoned, and one side would win and be weakened, and then he would make his move. On the surgeon's table the pain had brought back a fever dream from the time [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) pulled him back from the edge of death. Now he answered to no master at all, and he meant to sit out the end of the game.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-alone", "Alone",
                    listOf(
                        "When his spirit's journey into the past went wrong, [Teferi](who/teferi) woke naked on a beach, whole again and cut off from Kaya. When he reached for the sun to make it set, time would not answer him. He had come home to Zhalfir, the land he had phased out of the Multiverse long ago to save it, nearly four hundred years after he left it. Joining a caravan under a false name, he found the guards were bandits who had killed the real ones. Their leader, Eshe, recognised him. She lined up the caravanners and had them name their dead in Zhalfir's wars, and then named him, the snake who slipped away.",
                        "Teferi stopped time as her spear came for his heart. He told her, though she could barely hear him, about Subira, the woman who had loved him in spite of what he had done, and said that love saves a soul but does not heal a land. He took her spearhead in his hand and let it cut him, and promised to see Zhalfir safe through what came next. Then he let time go, and walked away.",
                        "Later he hauled fishing nets in a river village, quiet and penitent. A young woman named Oyana told him Zhalfir was ready, that its warclans would stop the Phyrexians. Teferi said no one was ready, not even the brave. Looking up, he could just see the void behind the sky, like bare stone under thin paint, the plane still cut off from everything. The thread from here runs into the [invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse), where Zhalfir finally came back.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One Commander"),
                StorySource("Story", "A Hollow Body, by Aysha U. Farah, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Cinders, by Cassandra Khaw, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "A Man of Parts, by Reinhardt Suarez, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Alone, by Reinhardt Suarez, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-factions", "Factions",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-machine-orthodoxy", "Machine Orthodoxy",
                    listOf(
                        "The white Phyrexians of the Machine Orthodoxy are a church. They follow scripture called the Argent Etchings with a literal faith and dream of a Multiverse gathered into one perfect body. They split into sects. The Flesh Singularity flays creatures and sews them together into literal unity. The Porcelain Legion grafts plates of white metal into bodies. The Disciples of [Karn](who/karn) once kept the golem captive, hoping to wring leadership out of his madness. Their plates are cracked white like old porcelain, and by now many have grown teeth.",
                        "At the top of the church is [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn), the Grand Cenobite and Mother of Machines, who speaks of herself as we. Under her stands Atraxa, the Grand Unifier, once a Mirran angel who held a Mirran retreat alone until she was captured. Norn honoured her stubbornness with compleation and let three other praetors add their gifts, and charged her with unifying the plane while Norn tended the tree. As the invasion gathered, the Orthodoxy became the heart of the Machine Legion, and the white Alabaster Host marched at its front singing hymns.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("She proudly carries the weight of Phyrexia's creed, both figuratively and literally.", "Testament Bearer"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-progress-engine", "Progress Engine",
                    listOf(
                        "The blue Phyrexians of the Progress Engine belong to Jin-Gitaxias, the Core Augur, and they pursue what they call the Great Synthesis in the Surgical Bay. They are the most organized of the factions, with at least ten ranks, and their laboratories are slaughterhouses. Among their great works is the Meldweb, the brains of hundreds of neurok and vedalken wired together in the old Pools of Insight. Mechanical eyes stare from every wall and most bodies, and optic cables hang off them like tentacles.",
                        "Jin-Gitaxias had been the first praetor to go over to Norn. His task for the war was the hardest one, to learn how to compleat a planeswalker without destroying the soul that holds the spark. He found the answer by experimenting on the kami of Kamigawa, and Tamiyo was the first to lie on his table. He was cut in half for it by the Wanderer and rebuilt on New Phyrexia. In this set he is the cold surgeon who gives Tezzeret his darksteel body and plans to soak it in oil until it turns to blightsteel.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Please, for science, tell me how it feels. Leave nothing out.\"", "Prologue to Phyresis"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-steel-thanes", "The Steel Thanes",
                    listOf(
                        "The black Phyrexians are the fleshiest of the plane and the most traditional, out for slaughter and enslavement. Their sphere is ruled by Sheoldred, the Whispering One, over a squabbling court of warlords called the Steel Thanes, all fighting to succeed her. Kraynox the Deep Thane lords over the oil, Roxith the Thane of Rot over flesh, Azax-Azog the Demon Thane rules by fear, Vraan leads vampire assassins, Thrissik the Writhing Thane builds cairns of captured mages to bring forth his Destroyer, and Geth the Thane of Contracts sells his protection.",
                        "Sheoldred came home from Dominaria, and Norn named her an apostate. During the strike team's descent she, Roxith, Geth and Vraan threw in with Urabrask's rebellion, while the others grabbed whatever ground was left undefended. It came to nothing. Geth was killed by Ixhel, and Sheoldred was executed soon after the planeswalkers left, long before the invasion was at its height. Once she was gone, her Etched Host was divided among the thanes who were left, each eager to win Norn's approval through cruelty. The card {Sheoldred's Edict} gives the praetor's old verdict on anything in her way, and {Phyrexian Obliterator} lets her speak her mind about the white praetor's servants.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Show me what Norn's sycophants are hiding under all that pretty porcelain.\"", "Sheoldred, Phyrexian Obliterator"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-quiet-furnace", "Quiet Furnace",
                    listOf(
                        "The red Phyrexians of the Quiet Furnace built the Furnace Layer before anything else, on the pattern of a similar layer in old Phyrexia, and tend its forges still, building weapons and recycling failed Phyrexians and Mirrans alike. They glow from inside through the plates of what was once their skin. Unlike every other Phyrexian, they keep strong feelings and some sense of themselves, and that left room for something no other faction has, a kind of empathy for the people they conquered.",
                        "Their praetor Urabrask, the Quiet Furnace, let the Mirran survivors shelter in his sphere and treated them as if they did not exist, and he barred the other factions from the Furnace to keep the secret. He went much further than that. He went to New Capenna through Tezzeret's bridge, found Vivien Reid and the Halo that poisons Phyrexians, and led a rebellion against Norn while the strike team climbed down. When the invasion came he refused to take part, and Norn simply turned his Furnace Host loose on the Multiverse with no one leading it.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Where once there was apathy, Urabrask brought passion, and the Great Work truly began.\"", "Red Sun's Twilight"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-vicious-swarm", "Vicious Swarm",
                    listOf(
                        "The green Phyrexians of the Vicious Swarm believe what every Phyrexian believes, that flesh is weak, but they think the new Phyrexia should be made the way nature makes things, by predators. The strong eat the weak and the strongest are improved. Thought is a curse to them, a poor thing next to instinct, and they have no organization at all, only a mass of creatures killing each other to find the best. Their corruption grows as copper plates streaked with verdigris.",
                        "Vorinclex, the Voice of Hunger, is their praetor. He stalked Kaldheim's realms for prey and brought home the tyrite that grew into the Invasion Tree. But the real power in the Hunter Maze is Glissa, the elf who once made Mirrodin's fifth sun and who fights Vorinclex for joy. Ezuri, once leader of the Viridian elves, is one of theirs now too, and he has a word for his old friends. On the cards the Swarm is the colour of copper and hunger, {Zopandrel, Hunger Dominus} doubling everything it fights beside and toxic beasts trampling through the Maze. When the invasion came they marched as the Copper Host, and they compleated fewer victims than any other host, because they only kept the strongest.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Ah, my old friends. Have you decided at last to stop clinging to the past and join me in the grand pursuit of perfection?\"", "Ezuri, Stalker of Spheres"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-mirran-resistance", "Mirran Resistance",
                    listOf(
                        "What is left of Mirrodin fights on as the Mirran Resistance, mostly Auriok, Vulshok, goblins and leonin with the last few moriok, neurok, sylvok, elves, loxodons and vedalken. It has no central command. Koth, the Vulshok geomancer, leads it most visibly, and its camps have their own leaders. Melira kept the camp called Seedling, the leonin kha Kemba kept Lowlight near the Cave of Light, and Slagmaw, the biggest and safest, sits inside the body of something that was once alive. Many in the camps are dying of late phyresis, tended by the less sick.",
                        "Because they are always hunted, everyone trains to fight, children and elders included, and they live by scavenging and moving on. They make hexgold from Glimmervoid plates and blinkmoth serum. Their equipment carries their history. The Rebels the set makes when Equipment enters are these people, picking up a dead parent's helm and walking into the fight. Koth's people do not expect to win back the world they had. They fight so that no other plane suffers what theirs did. When the war was over, fewer than twenty Mirrans escaped to Zhalfir.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"My mother wore this into battle against the first Phyrexians. Now I wear it in her honor.\"", "Goldwarden's Helm"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Story", "A Hollow Body, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexia (plane)"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Atraxa"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-planeswalkers", "The Planeswalkers",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-compleated", "The Compleated",
                    listOf(
                        "Five of the set's ten planeswalkers are compleated, and each card carries the new Compleated keyword and Phyrexian mana in its cost. {Nahiri, the Unforgiving} is red and white, the lithomancer remade with blades for hands, who loots and brings back creatures or Equipment from the graveyard as hasty copies. {Jace, the Perfected Mind} is mono-blue and mills, the mind that held the sylex now turned to emptying libraries. {Vraska, Betrayal's Sting} draws and proliferates and can turn a creature into Treasure, and her ultimate tops a player up to nine poison counters, which is about as close to the end as a game can get.",
                        "{Lukka, Bound to Ruin} is red and green, making 3/3 Phyrexian Beasts with toxic and flinging damage equal to his biggest creature's power. {Nissa, Ascended Animist} is the most expensive, with two Phyrexian green symbols, making a Horror as big as her loyalty every turn. Each enters with fewer loyalty counters for every Phyrexian symbol paid with life. They are cheaper the more you are willing to lose, which suits them. The story of how each fell is told in [the Dross Pits](one-the-dross-pits), [the Hunter Maze](one-the-hunter-maze) and [the end](one-bring-the-ending), and when the invasion came, [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) sent several of them home to conquer the worlds they had come from.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-still-free", "Those Still Free",
                    listOf(
                        "The other five are the planeswalkers who came out of the descent still themselves. {Koth, Fire of Resistance} is mono-red, fetching Mountains and burning creatures for each one, with an emblem that turns every Mountain into four damage. {Kaya, Intangible Slayer} is a seven mana white and black planeswalker with hexproof, who drains, draws and exiles a creature or enchantment while leaving a ghostly 1/1 copy behind. {Kaito, Dancing Shadow} is blue and black, bouncing his own attackers to activate twice in a turn and making deathtouch Drones.",
                        "{Tyvar, Jubilant Brawler} lets creatures use their abilities as if they had haste and returns small creatures from the graveyard. {The Eternal Wanderer} costs six, makes double strike Samurai, blinks a creature or artifact out until the next end step, and can't be attacked by more than one creature at a time. It suits a woman who could barely stay on the plane. Elspeth, who led them, is remembered by {Vanish into Eternity}, the moment she carried the bomb away. Set beside the five compleated cards, these five show what [Jace](who/jace) and [Nahiri](who/nahiri) and the others might have stayed.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-what-ifs", "What If",
                    listOf(
                        "The set's borderless manga showcase drew all ten planeswalkers as compleated Phyrexians in Japanese anime style, the kind of art War of the Spark had used for its planeswalkers. Only five of those pictures are true to the story. The other five are a what if. They show Koth, Kaya, Kaito, Tyvar and the Wanderer as they would have looked if the descent had gone worse, porcelain and oil grown over faces that in the story stayed their own.",
                        "Players who wanted the five survivors as they truly are could take the regular frames, and those who liked the darker version could chase the manga. Either way the art makes the same point the story does. Anyone who walked into New Phyrexia was a single scratch away from becoming one of them, as Nahiri learned from one cut on the back of her neck, and [Karn](who/karn)'s plan cost half of the planeswalkers on the cards. Five went down and five came back, and the five who came back carried the news to a Multiverse that was already being invaded.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Story", "Assault on New Phyrexia, Episodes 1 to 5, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-mechanics", "Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-toxic-and-poison", "Toxic and Poison",
                    listOf(
                        "Poison counters are back. A player with ten loses the game, and the set's main way of giving them is a new keyword, toxic. A creature with toxic 1 or toxic 2 gives that many poison counters to any player it deals combat damage to, on top of the damage itself. It works like lifelink rather than a trigger, so the counters arrive at the same time as the damage. It is a reworking of the old poisonous keyword, which was a triggered ability, and unlike infect from the last visit to the plane, it leaves the ordinary damage in place.",
                        "Toxic creatures still hit for real damage, so a player can race on two fronts at once. The 1/1 Phyrexian Mite tokens that swarm the set all have toxic 1 and can't block. Before this set only {Caress of Phyrexia} gave poison counters directly. Now five cards do, among them {Prologue to Phyresis}, {Infectious Bite} and {Vraska's Fall}, so a poison deck can finish the last few counters without connecting in combat. The new creature type Mite joined the game here, and more than 120 cards in the set carry the Phyrexian creature type.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-corrupted", "Corrupted",
                    listOf(
                        "Corrupted is an ability word, the set's version of threshold. A corrupted ability turns on once an opponent has three or more poison counters. {Bring the Ending} is a soft counterspell that becomes a hard one. {Skrelv's Hive} makes a Mite every upkeep and, once corrupted, gives all your toxic creatures lifelink. {The Seedcore} pumps a 1/1 creature, and {Ixhel, Scion of Atraxa} lets you steal the top card of every corrupted opponent's library each turn.",
                        "The point of corrupted is that a poison counter is never wasted. A single toxic hit early in the game sets up a threshold the rest of your deck cares about, even if you never get to ten. White and black was the corrupted archetype in Limited. A deck could put three counters on an opponent with a few Mites and a spell or two and then win on damage, using the corrupted bonuses to do it. It also made counting poison a matter of real interest for every player at the table, which is what a mechanic about slow infection ought to do.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-oil-and-proliferate", "Oil and Proliferate",
                    listOf(
                        "Proliferate returned, the most Phyrexian mechanic there is, a rule that makes whatever is already spreading spread faster. To proliferate you choose any number of permanents and players and give each one more of every kind of counter already there, so poison grows, loyalty grows, and everything else grows with it. {Bloated Contaminator} proliferates when it hits, {Blightbelly Rat} when it dies, and {Ezuri, Stalker of Spheres} draws a card every time you do.",
                        "To give proliferate something new to grow, the designers left out +1/+1 and -1/-1 counters entirely and invented oil counters. Oil means nothing by itself. Each card that uses it says what it does. {Urabrask's Forge} adds oil every turn and makes a bigger hasty Horror each time. {Archfiend of the Dross} enters with four oil counters, loses one each upkeep, and makes you lose the game when the last is gone, an alternate-lose card. {Evolving Adaptive} grows like evolve, using oil. That makes this the only premier set with no counters at all that change power and toughness.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-for-mirrodin", "For Mirrodin",
                    listOf(
                        "The Mirrans got a keyword of their own. For Mirrodin is a keyword on Equipment. When the Equipment enters, you create a 2/2 red Rebel creature token and attach the Equipment to it. It works like living weapon from the last visit to the plane, a weapon that comes with a body, but the body this time is a person. Rebel was back as a creature type for the first time since Time Spiral, and only four nontoken cards in the set carry it, the handful of Mirrans left alive.",
                        "{Hexgold Halberd} gives first strike and trample on your turn. {Mirran Bardiche} is a big +2/+1 with vigilance. {Bladehold War-Whip} grants double strike and cheapens your other equip costs. Red and white was the Equipment deck in Limited. Battle cry also returned from the Scars of Mirrodin block, on cards like {Ria Ivor, Bane of Bladehold}, and the effect of every For Mirrodin card is the same small story, somebody picking up a weapon off the ground and joining the fight. Kill the Rebel and the weapon stays behind for the next one to pick up.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Finally, a safe way to punch the rotters in the face.\"", "Barbed Batterfist"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-phyrexian-mana", "Phyrexian Mana",
                    listOf(
                        "Phyrexian mana, a symbol that can be paid with coloured mana or with 2 life, came back from the old New Phyrexia set, but only in activation costs. The one exception is the compleated planeswalkers, which have Phyrexian symbols, some of them hybrid, in their mana costs and enter with fewer loyalty counters for each one paid with life. The Domini use it to put indestructible counters on themselves. {Skrelv, Defector Mite} pays it to hand out toxic and protection. Paying life instead of mana is the Phyrexian bargain in miniature, power now and the cost carved out of your own body.",
                        "The set also introduced Sphere, a new land type for the nine layers of the plane. The five common Spheres enter tapped and can be cashed in for a card. {Mirrex}, {The Monumental Facade}, {The Mycosynth Gardens} and {The Seedcore} are the rare ones. And {Atraxa, Grand Unifier} lists every card type in its reminder text, including one nobody had ever seen, battle. It was a teaser for March of the Machine, which arrived two months later with the first battles in the game.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Story", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One Direction, Parts 1 and 2, by Mark Rosewater, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-cards", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-norn-and-atraxa", "Norn and Atraxa",
                    listOf(
                        "The face of the set is {Elesh Norn, Mother of Machines}, a five mana 4/7 with vigilance. When a permanent enters, your abilities that trigger from it trigger an additional time, and your opponents' don't trigger at all. In Commander it doubles every enters ability you have and shuts off everyone else's, and in a game of four players that is a great deal of power. She came in six different versions in this set alone, and [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) herself is on the art of every product in the line, key art by Magali Villeneuve.",
                        "{Atraxa, Grand Unifier} is the other one. Seven mana in four colours for a 7/7 with flying, vigilance, deathtouch and lifelink, and when she enters you reveal the top ten cards and take one of each card type. In constructed formats she became the best reanimation target since Griselbrand, drawing almost as many cards without his life cost, and she is a natural four colour commander too. Her card is also the first to say battle. In the story she is Norn's general, the angel who unified the plane while the Mother tended her tree.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Drown, so you may breathe again.\"", "Atraxa, Norn's Wellspring"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-domini", "The Domini",
                    listOf(
                        "The Domini are parts of New Phyrexia that woke up. When the oil began remaking the plane, pieces of the land shook themselves and began to wander, and nobody truly knows whether they think or want anything the way other Phyrexians do. Each is a living monument to the ideals of one praetor. The set has one in each colour, and each doubles something. {Mondrak, Glory Dominus} doubles tokens. {Tekuthal, Inquiry Dominus} makes you proliferate twice. {Drivnod, Carnage Dominus} doubles death triggers. {Solphim, Mayhem Dominus} doubles noncombat damage to opponents and their permanents. {Zopandrel, Hunger Dominus} doubles the power and toughness of your creatures every combat.",
                        "Each also pays two Phyrexian mana and a heavy cost of sacrifice, discard or exile to put an indestructible counter on itself. They suit Commander, where doubling effects are prized. Mondrak belongs in token decks, Drivnod in decks that sacrifice their own creatures, and Tekuthal in anything with counters. They are the set's quiet monsters, giant and strange. In the side story A Hollow Body, the Dominus of Pain stands in the Dross Pits hung with impaled bodies, drinking their screams, and even the angel Ixhel admits she does not understand it.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-cycles", "Cycles",
                    listOf(
                        "The Suns' Twilight sorceries finish a three-part story told across visits to Mirrodin. Fifth Dawn showed the suns at dawn and Mirrodin Besieged at their zenith. Now they set. Each is an X spell that does more at X of five or more, and each shows a monument to a praetor. {Black Sun's Twilight} shrinks a creature and can reanimate. {Green Sun's Twilight} finds a creature and a land and can put them straight into play. {White Sun's Twilight}, {Blue Sun's Twilight} and {Red Sun's Twilight} round it out. The inscriptions on their monuments tell Phyrexia's own version of history, in which the black praetor brought back the will of [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth) and each of the others brought a gift.",
                        "The Skullbombs finish another trio, after the Spellbombs of Mirrodin and Scars, each a common artifact you sacrifice to draw a card, cheaply or for a coloured effect. {Basilica Skullbomb} is the white one, shaped faintly like the praetor whose sphere it is named for. The oil containers, {Norn's Wellspring} and {Churning Reservoir} among them, store oil for later. And the allied fast lands from Scars of Mirrodin came back, {Seachrome Coast} and its four siblings, beside reprints of {Phyrexian Obliterator} and {Phyrexian Arena}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Where once there was complacence, Sheoldred brought ambition, and soon the will of Yawgmoth reigned.\"", "Black Sun's Twilight"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-legends", "Legends",
                    listOf(
                        "Ten rare legendary creatures cover the ten two-colour pairs. {Melira, the Living Cure} stops you taking more than one poison counter a turn and can bring back a creature or artifact after it dies. {Glissa Sunslayer} has first strike and deathtouch and can strip three counters off anything. {Venser, Corpse Puppet} is Venser's own compleated body, making a Golem token called The Hollow Sentinel when you proliferate. {Jor Kadeen, First Goldwarden} grows with equipped creatures, {Ria Ivor, Bane of Bladehold} turns combat damage into Mites, and {Malcator, Purity Overseer} makes Golems.",
                        "The Goliaths are the rest, {Kethek, Crucible Goliath}, {Migloz, Maze Crusher} and {Ovika, Enigma Goliath}, beside {Ezuri, Stalker of Spheres}. Among the mono-coloured legends, {Thrun, Breaker of Silence} is the last troll, a giant nobody else's spells can touch. {Kemba, Kha Enduring} straps Equipment onto Cats. {Slobad, Iron Goblin} turns artifacts into mana, and {Geth, Thane of Contracts} brings creatures back for a price. {Skrelv, Defector Mite} is the Phyrexian who switched sides, and {Karumonix, the Rat King} was the foil bundle promo. Between them the legends cover almost every corner of the war, the resistance, the compleated and the strange creatures in between.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-limited", "Drafting the Set",
                    listOf(
                        "Each of the ten colour pairs had a theme in Limited. White and blue played artifacts, blue and black proliferate control, black and red oil and sacrifice, red and green midrange oil, green and white toxic aggression. White and black played corrupted, blue and red oil with noncombat spells, black and green tried to win on poison, red and white equipped Rebels, and green and blue proliferated poison home. Each pair has a gold uncommon that points the way, like {Bladehold War-Whip}.",
                        "Under the themes ran a simple tension. Poison decks could ignore the opponent's life total, so every game was really two races, and corrupted cards rewarded a deck for getting a few counters on early even if it never meant to win that way. The commons were full of Mites, oil and Skullbombs, and the reprinted {Thrill of Possibility} and {Terramorphic Expanse} filled holes. The set was also sold in Jumpstart boosters, and the prerelease had players open two Jumpstart packs and shuffle them together alongside their kit. Each prerelease kit also held a stamped rare or mythic from the set.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Story", "A Hollow Body, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "one-commander", "Commander and Extras",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "one-corrupting-influence", "Corrupting Influence",
                    listOf(
                        "The set came with two Commander decks, designed by Ari Nieh and Bella Guo, each with ten new cards. Corrupting Influence is white, black and green, led by {Ixhel, Scion of Atraxa}, a flying, vigilant angel with toxic 2 who exiles the top card of every corrupted opponent's library at your end step and lets you play them. Her partner in the story, {Vishgraz, the Doomhive}, is in the deck too, arriving with three Mites and growing for every poison counter your opponents have.",
                        "Commander players start at forty life, and poison does not care. Ten counters ends a game whether a player has forty life or four, which is why a poison deck can be so frightening at a Commander table. The deck is full of toxic creatures and proliferate, with cards like {Caress of Phyrexia}, {Phyresis Outbreak}, {Infectious Inquiry} and {Myr Convert}, and Ixhel's ability means every hit pays off twice, once in poison and once in stolen cards. Among the eight extra Commander cards found only in Set and Collector Boosters is {Monumental Corruption}, which carries a line from Kaya.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("\"Funny, I always thought the Phyrexians were a heartless lot.\"", "Kaya, Monumental Corruption"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-rebellion-rising", "Rebellion Rising",
                    listOf(
                        "Rebellion Rising is red and white, the Mirran Resistance's deck, led by {Neyali, Suns' Vanguard}. Attacking tokens you control have double strike, and whenever tokens attack a player you exile the top card of your library and may play it during any turn you attacked with a token. The firebird {Otharri, Suns' Glory} rides with her as in the story, making more Rebels each time it attacks and rising from the graveyard by tapping one of them. Neyali's ability rewards going wide, since every token attacking with double strike hits twice, and the cards she exiles keep the deck fed while the army grows.",
                        "The deck makes many small soldiers and straps Equipment to them. It reprints {Elspeth Tirel} from Scars of Mirrodin, {Call the Coppercoats}, and the {Goldwardens' Gambit} that shows Neyali's cell striking at her command, and it adds new cards like {Kemba's Banner}. The eight Commander cards found only in boosters include {Synthesis Pod}, {Chiss-Goria, Forge Tyrant} and {Urtet, Remnant of Memnarch}, a myr lord named for Mirrodin's old warden.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("At Neyali's command, they took position. At Otharri's signal, they struck.", "Goldwardens' Gambit"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-treatments", "Showcase Treatments",
                    listOf(
                        "The set's special frames were some of the strangest ever printed. The ichor showcase rendered cards in black and white, as if drawn in oil. The borderless manga cards turned planeswalkers and characters into compleated Phyrexians, five of them true and five of them what ifs. Five planeswalkers came in Phyrexian language versions with their names and rules in the Phyrexian script. The Step-and-Compleat foil gave some Collector Booster cards a texture of their own, and the Compleat Edition of the bundle held Oil Slick Raised Foil cards that look as if they had been dipped in glistening oil.",
                        "The praetors of earlier sets came back too, in borderless concept art by Richard Whitters, found in all three booster types. There were the usual extended art cards, borderless dual lands, and five full art Phyrexian panorama basics and five Phyrexianized full art basics in the main set. The Compleat bundle carried a Phyrexian language {Phyrexian Arena}, the winner of a Store Championship took home a full art {Koth, Fire of Resistance}, and the Buy-a-Box promo was an alternate art {Green Sun's Twilight}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "one-the-launch", "The Launch",
                    listOf(
                        "The set's vision design was led by Mark Rosewater and its set design by Adam Prosak and Erik Lauer together, under the codename Lacrosse. Its launch had its share of trouble. In October 2022, months before release, several showcase cards leaked online, revealing the Step-and-Compleat foil and some of the mechanics. Then a week before previews began, nearly all of the set's rares turned up in packs of Dominaria Remastered and were spoiled in a rush.",
                        "It was also the set where Store Championships replaced Game Day. Prerelease kits had a small chance of holding a spindown life counter printed in Phyrexian numerals, and in parts of Europe a store purchase could earn a green ten-sided die with the Phyrexian symbol in place of the ten, a poison counter you could hold in your hand. Beadle and Grimm's sold a box called Behold New Phyrexia made to look as if it had come out of the Fair Basilica. The Compleat Edition of the bundle followed in early March, and in April came the invasion the whole set had been promising.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One Commander"),
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Phyrexia: All Will Be One/Commander"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
